// Per-node capacity / utilization math for the node-capacity warning feature.
//
// Plain (non-"use server") module — safe to import from admin server components
// (the Compute-nodes and Web-hosting pages render bars off it) AND from the
// worker sweep (worker/monitor.ts → nodeCapacitySweep raises/clears NodeAlerts
// off it). No mutations here; the alert lifecycle lives in the worker so this
// file stays a pure-ish reader other surfaces can share, exactly like the
// read-only helpers in lib/monitoring.ts.
//
// TWO NODE TYPES, ONE MODEL. Nxeon schedules customer VMs onto Proxmox `Node`s
// and shared-hosting accounts onto `HostingNode`s. Both fill up and both refuse
// new work when they do, so both are reduced to the same shape: a list of
// per-DIMENSION utilization percentages (CPU/RAM/DISK for Proxmox, ACCOUNTS/DISK
// for hosting) that the thresholds below turn into OK / WARN / CRITICAL.
//
// ALLOCATION vs REAL USAGE. For each dimension we take the WORSE of two numbers:
//   - allocation: what the DB has COMMITTED to the node (sum of every server's
//     plan RAM/disk/vCPU, or the account count). This is what makes the NEXT
//     provision fail — a node can be 100% committed while barely warm.
//   - real usage: what Proxmox actually reports in use right now (RAM/CPU/disk),
//     which is what makes a node DEGRADE.
// Warning on max(alloc, real) means we fire on whichever ceiling is hit first,
// which is the honest "add a node before this bites" signal the feature exists
// to give.

